1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,168
Right now, particles from deep space are
slamming into the top of our atmosphere.

2
00:00:05,368 --> 00:00:09,557
Each collision sets off a shower, and
among the debris are muons: heavier

3
00:00:09,607 --> 00:00:10,950
cousins of the electron.

4
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:15,466
A muon lives for just two millionths of a
second.

5
00:00:15,666 --> 00:00:18,291
That should not be long enough to reach
the ground.

6
00:00:18,491 --> 00:00:22,728
But they move so close to the speed of
light that time stretches for them, and

7
00:00:22,778 --> 00:00:26,190
about one crosses every square centimetre
of Earth each minute.

8
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:32,288
To catch them, we use tiles of special
plastic that glow when a muon passes

9
00:00:32,338 --> 00:00:32,757
through.

10
00:00:32,957 --> 00:00:37,744
Optical fibres carry that faint flash to a
silicon photomultiplier, a sensor so

11
00:00:37,794 --> 00:00:41,050
sensitive it can respond to single
particles of light.

12
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:46,192
But these sensors also fire at random, all
on their own.

13
00:00:46,242 --> 00:00:51,049
So we stack three tiles and only count a
muon when two of them flash within the

14
00:00:51,099 --> 00:00:55,966
same few hundred billionths of a second.
Random noise almost never lines up like

15
00:00:56,016 --> 00:00:57,380
that.
A real muon does.

16
00:00:59,130 --> 00:01:02,620
Count them minute by minute, and something
surprising appears.

17
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:07,737
When air pressure rises, there is more air
overhead to stop muons, and the count

18
00:01:07,787 --> 00:01:08,110
drops.

19
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:14,338
A small detector, logging on its own,
watching particles made by the universe.

20
00:01:14,538 --> 00:01:17,641
Even this music was shaped by the muons it
counted.

21
00:01:17,841 --> 00:01:19,180
This is gLOWCOST.

